  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nLATE TONIGHT--WEDNESDAY MORNING...Similar to this morning, expecting \na smattering of hit-or-miss showers and/or sprinkles late tonight \nthrough Wednesday morning across the region, as a low-amplitude \nshortwave meanders through Mid-America. High cloud bases and limited \nmoisture/buoyancy and expected lift will support only light rainfall \namounts. Can't completely rule out a few stray lightning strikes \ngiven lower-end buoyancy, but most locations will remain thunder-\nfree, and severe weather is absolutely not expected.\n\nLATE SATURDAY--EARLY NEXT WEEK...Per deterministic and ensemble \nconsensus, a deep western CONUS upper trough approaching from the \nwest in concert with a cold front approaching from the northwest \nshould support increasing shower/thunderstorm chances as we head \ninto later Saturday and persisting into early next week. Run-to-run \nand model-to-model consistency is fairly poor this far out, so \nuncertainty remains high surrounding timing, coverage, and rainfall \namounts. Stay tuned as we refine forecast details in the coming \ndays.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nBuilding heights/thickness and persistent southerly flow will \nsupport a warming trend to mostly above average temperatures as the \nweek progresses, and possibly persisting into the weekend. Forecast \nhighs climbing into the mid-upper 70s look probable by mid-late \nweek, with forecast lows in the 50s. Additionally, persistent High \nPlains lee troughing will support breezy south winds, strongest \nFriday and Saturday when gusts could top 30-35 mph.\n\nAs we head into next week, an eventual cool down looks probable,\nas model consensus supports a deepening central and eastern\nCONUS upper trough, and associated Canadian high pressure\nsettling south into the Heartland.",
